From e9af7c1bb9bb53eee659a9fccc0d514ef993a41a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 08:11:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix silent 401/403 bodies and dropped backend error detail

Two real bugs found during hardware validation, both traced to their
actual root cause rather than patched at the symptom.

1. Empty 401/403 responses

app.UseAuthorization() was already correctly placed after
UseAuthentication(), but a failed authentication/authorization check
never throws - it short-circuits the pipeline by setting the status code
directly. GlobalExceptionMiddleware only reacts to thrown exceptions, so
it never saw these at all; the caller got a bare empty 401/403 with no
body, on every single auth failure since the foundation phase.

Fixed with JwtBearerEvents.OnChallenge/OnForbidden on the JwtBearer
registration in Program.cs, writing the standard ApiResponse<T> envelope
directly to the response. OnChallenge calls HandleResponse() first so
the default (empty) challenge behavior doesn't also run afterward and
clobber what was just written. Two new error codes: Machine.
Unauthenticated (no/invalid/expired/wrong-key token) and Machine.
Forbidden (a structurally valid token that fails the PrintAgentOnly
policy's claim check - i.e. not a Print Agent token at all).

Extracted the shared "localize this MachineError and write it straight
to an HttpResponse" logic - previously duplicated inside
GlobalExceptionMiddleware - into Common/ErrorResponseWriter, since the
new auth event handlers need the exact same thing and duplicating it a
third time was the wrong call. GlobalExceptionMiddleware now just calls
it.

2. Backend-supplied error detail silently dropped

The actual bug: MachineError.MessageArg was designed to carry contextual
detail (e.g. the Inventory API's specific rejection reason) for
substitution into a resx {0} placeholder - but every single entry in
both Messages.resx and Messages.ar.resx was missing {0} entirely. Since
this catalogue existed. IStringLocalizer's format-args indexer calls
string.Format under the hood, which silently ignores an argument that
has no placeholder to fill rather than erroring - so "insufficient
stock" (or whatever the Inventory API actually said) was being computed,
passed all the way to the presentation edge, and then thrown away there,
every time, for every error code that carries a detail.

Fixed by not relying on resx placeholder substitution at all:
MachineError.ToApiError() and both presentation-edge consumers
(LocalizeErrorResultFilter, ErrorResponseWriter) now localize the base
message by code with no format args, then append MessageArg in code via
a new shared ApiError.ComposeMessage(base, detail) helper. This also
fixes the same bug in the English default path, which had it too
(ToApiError() was setting Message = Message, never appending MessageArg
at all, independent of localization) - so the fix holds even if a
future error code's resx entry is missing or the requested culture isn't
covered.

No dotnet SDK or NuGet access in the authoring environment. Verified by
a full-repo symbol-table cross-check (same four already-known false
positives as every prior phase - properties literally named Error on
four unrelated types, confirmed again by grep, nothing new), brace
balance across every touched file, JSON/XML validity on appsettings.json
and both resx files, and by tracing the exact reported scenario
(Backend Call #1's insufficient-stock rejection) through
MachineController.Print -> MachineErrors.BackendRejected ->
MachineError.ToApiError/the two consumers by hand to confirm the detail
survives every hop. Please build locally and re-run both the auth-
failure and insufficient-stock scenarios against a real request before
considering this closed.
